// Exercise: write a faster version of `multiply_matrices_jazz` (`matrix.jazz`)
// by translating the assembly that `gcc -O3` generates for `matrix.c`. The
// listing generated by GCC 13 is in `matrix_O3_gcc13.s` (`make` also writes
// the one from your GCC to `matrixO3.s`; it may use other registers).
//
// Start by understanding how the listing differs from `matrix.jazz`:
// - which registers hold pointers into `a`, `b` and `c`, and by how much they
//   are incremented (`%rdi` holds `n * 8`, the size of a row in bytes)?
// - how are the three loops tested? GCC writes them as
//   `do { ... } while (...)` loops, which in Jasmin are
//   `while { ... } (cond)`.
//
// Then translate it, naming your variables after the registers (rax, rbx, ...)
// to keep the correspondence obvious. Useful syntax:
//   leaq 0(,%rcx,8), %rdi     rdi = #LEA(n * 8);
//   movq (%rax), %rdx         rdx = [rax];
//   imulq (%rcx), %rdx        rdx *= [rcx];
//   movq %rsi, (%rbx,%r10,8)  [rbx + r10 * 8] = rsi;
//
// Run `make run` to check your function and compare the cycle counts. The
// solution is in `matrix_optimized_solution.jazz`.

